# A closure of the frame has one more word behind its captures: the address of its copy in the heap, or nothing. A slot of the frame is used again, by the next turn, the next loan and the next call, so every making zeroes the word.
# function twice(job) { return job() + job(); }  function once(k) { return twice(() => k) + 1; }  function turns(k) { let got = k; while (got) got = twice(() => k); return got; }
# function nine(k) { return twice(() => k + k + k + k + k + k + k + k + k) + 1; }, with a capture for each mention.  publish(once, turns, nine);
# Outside of loops a slim closure is filled by the stub, which knows the number of captures and zeroes the word itself: the code stores nothing there.
# RUN(STUB): emit f2 mechanisms=all-Inlining
# STUB-LABEL: unit f2:Body
# STUB-NOT: = Store({{*}}frameSlots
# STUB: = Return(
# In a loop the code fills the closure itself: header, word, one capture at 16, the word at 24.
# RUN(LOOP): emit f3 mechanisms=all-Inlining
# LOOP-LABEL: unit f3:Body
# LOOP: = Store({{*}}offset = 24, frameSlots
# A closure with nine captures has the full form, which the code always fills itself: the captures begin at 40, the word lies at 112.
# RUN(FULL): emit f4 mechanisms=all-Inlining
# FULL-LABEL: unit f4:Body
# FULL: = Store({{*}}offset = 112, frameSlots
